        textProvided: "Whip me the stunted bards and burn their verse!<br>Misshapen goblins, whipping-boys of fate<br>Abortions, whose pale sneer and maniac curse<br>Sit well on lips whose only love is hate.<br><br>When in the stricken pass of Roncevaux<br>The good knight Roland, sorely battle-worn<br>Among the paynim traitors, will not blow<br>One blast of succour on his ivory horn.<br><br>When in his deathly thirst and burning pain<br>At Zutphen by the Spanish ball o’erthrown,<br>Young Sydney gives the proffered cup again<br>To one whose need is greater than his own;<br><br>When from the tent where crouching mortals fight<br>Their desperate cause with death: and all about<br>Rages the horror of the undawning night,<br>A very gallant gentleman goes out:<br><br>I laugh to scorn philosophers that jibe,<br>Poets and satirists with venomed pen,<br>Cynics and priests and all the embittered tribe<br>That curse through dwarfish lips the sons of men.<br><br>[Lewis’s note: And God saw that the wickedness of man was great ... and that every imagination of his heart was but of evil, and that continually-Genesis. There is no more pitiful thing that moves on the face of all-nurturing earth than the sons of man-Homer. The most pernicious race of little vermin-Swift]",
