        textProvided: "If fragrant wealth of tangled hair<br>And living things all warm and fair,<br>Dure not beyond a moment’s space<br>But waste and wither everywhere;<br><br>Then were it better far to mould<br>Or chisel from refined gold<br>A faultless limb and living face<br>Whose tale was not so swiftly told;<br><br>And wiser were the Gods to set<br>A little golden statuette<br>To dream forever in the sun<br>And feel no taint of mortal fret,<br><br>Than thus to prison and in-mesh<br>A spirit in unquiet flesh,-<br>A thing whose work is never done<br>Whose hunger dies to wax afresh,<br><br>We envy sore this image sweet,<br>The dance of its unwearied feet<br>Frozen in joy, its eyes afar<br>Still set its unknown love to greet.<br><br>We deem them wise that wrought thee so!<br>But of their counsel who may know<br>That made us men-folk as we are,<br>Dear golden thing, unlearned of woe?",
