        printedIn: "Roger White, “C.S. Lewis’ Poem ‘Nearly They Stood’; A Variorum and Research Notes,” <i>The Chronicle of the Oxford University C.S. Lewis Society</i>",
        issueOrVolume: "vol. 6, no. 2",
        pageRange: "31-34",
        year: 2009,
        monthAndDay: "April",
        textProvided: "[From The Lewis Papers, 1933; unique passages in <b>bold</b>]<br><br>Nearly they stood who fall;<br>Themselves <b>as they</b> look back<br>See always in the track<br><b>The one false step, </b>where all<br>Even <b>yet, by lightest</b> swerve<br>Of foot not yet enslaved,<br>By smallest tremour of the smallest nerve,<br>Might have been saved.<br><br>Nearly they fell who stand,<br>And with <b>cold</b> after fear<br>Look back to mark how near<br>They grazed the Siren’s land,<br><b>Wondering that subtle</b> fate,<br>By threads so spidery fine,<br>The choice of ways so small, the event so great<br>Should thus entwine.<br><br>Therefore, <b>oh man, have</b> feat<br>Lest oldest fears <b>be</b> true,<br>Lest (while no bugle blew<br><b>Thy</b> mort, while heaven seemed clear)<br>Secure, <b>thou hadst gone</b> one hair’s<br>Breadth past <b>the hair’s</b> breadth bourne,<br>Which, being once crossed forever unawares,<br><b>Denies</b> return.<br><br>[The Barfield Papers Variant 1: from a letter to Barfield, Autumn 1926?; unique passages in <b>bold</b>]<br>Nearly they stood who fall.<br>Themselves when they look back <br>See always in the track<br>One torturing spot where all,<br>Even then, by minim swerve<br>Of foot not yet enslaved,<br>By smallest <b>movement</b> of the slenderest nerve,<br>Could have been saved.<br><br>Nearly they fell who stand,<br>And, with strange after-fear,<br>Look back to mark how near <br>They grazed the Siren’s land,<br>Hence wonder grows that thou<br><b>With</b> threads so spidery fine<br><b>That everlasting to this here and now</b><br><b>Shouldst</b> thus entwine.<br><br>Therefore at times I fear<br>Lest oldest fears <b>prove</b> true;<br>Lest—while no bugle blue<br>My mort, while heaven seemed clear,<br>Secure, I have stepped one hair’s<br>Breadth past some hair-breadth bourne,<br>Which, being once crossed forever unawares,<br>Forbids return.<br><br>[The Barfield Papers Variant 2: labeled “IV” in “Half Hours with Hamilton or Quiet Moments,” a collection of 14 poems by Lewis given to Barfield in the summer of 1930; unique passages in <b>bold</b>]<br><br>Nearly they stood who fall;<br>Themselves when they look back<br>See always in the track<br>One torturing spot where all<br>Even then, by minim swerve<br>Of foot not yet enslaved,<br>By <b>faintest moving</b> of the slenderest nerve,<br>Could have been saved.<br><br>Nearly they fell who stand;<br>And with strange after fear<br>Look back to mark how near <br>They grazed the Siren’s land.<br>Hence wonder grows that fate<br>By threads so spidery fine,<br>The choice of ways so small, the event so great,<br>Therefore at times I fear<br>Lest oldest fears are true,<br>Lest, while no bugle blew<br>My mort, while heaven seemed clear,<br>Secure, I have stepped one hair’s <br>-Breadth past some hair-breadth bourne,<br>Which, being once crossed forever unawares,<br>Forbids return.<br><br>[The Barfield Papers Variant 3: the first of two poems appearing on two typed pages under the heading ‘Hell. Two Poems’ probably given to Barfield by Lewis, unknown date; unique passages in <b>bold</b>]<br>Nearly they stood who fall.<br>Themselves when they look back<br>See always on the track<br>One torturing spot where all,<br>Even then, by minim swerve<br>Of foot not yet enslaved,<br>Or <b>smallest flexion</b> of the slenderest nerve,<br>Could have been saved.<br><br>Nearly they fell who stand;<br>And, with strange after-fear,<br>Look back to the mark how near<br>They grazed the Sirens land.<br>Hence wonder grows that Fate,<br>By threads so <b>cobweb</b> fine,<br>The choice of ways so small, the event so great,<br>Should thus entwine.<br><br>Therefore, at time, I fear<br>Lest oldest fears are true,<br>Lest—while no bugle blew<br>My mort, while heaven seemed clear—<br>Secure, I have stepped one hair’s<br>Breadth past some hair-breadth bourn,<br>Which, being once crossed forever, unawares,<br>Forbids return.",
